/** @page qore_modules Developing Qore Modules

    Copyright (C) 2003 - 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

    @section module_dev Qore Module Development

    Binary modules are shared libraries (`.so` / `.dylib` / `.dll`) that extend the %Qore language with new classes,
    functions, constants, and namespaces.

    Since %Qore 2.0, binary modules use Module API 2.0, which requires a single
    \c extern \c "C" module description function instead of the old-style exported global variables.

    @subsection module_header Module Description Function

    Every binary module must define exactly one \c extern \c "C" function named
    <tt>{module_name}_qore_module_desc</tt> that populates a @ref QoreModuleInfo struct.

    @note If the module name contains hyphens, they are converted to underscores when looking up the description
    function symbol.  For example, a module named \c "my-module" should define
    \c my_module_qore_module_desc(QoreModuleInfo&).

    @code{.cpp}
    extern "C" DLLEXPORT void mymodule_qore_module_desc(QoreModuleInfo& mod_info) {
        mod_info.name = "mymodule";
        mod_info.version = PACKAGE_VERSION;
        mod_info.desc = "My Qore module";
        mod_info.author = "Author Name <author@example.com>";
        mod_info.url = "http://example.com";
        mod_info.api_major = QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR;
        mod_info.api_minor = QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR;
        mod_info.init = mymodule_module_init;
        mod_info.ns_init = mymodule_module_ns_init;
        mod_info.del = mymodule_module_delete;
        mod_info.license = QL_MIT;
        mod_info.license_str = "MIT";
    }
    @endcode

    The @ref QoreModuleInfo struct has the following fields:
    - \c name — the module's feature name; must be unique across all loaded modules
    - \c version — the module version string
    - \c desc — a human-readable description
    - \c author — the module author(s)
    - \c url — a URL for the module
    - \c api_major / \c api_minor — must be set to @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR and @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR
    - \c init — the module initialization callback (@ref qore_module_init_t)
    - \c ns_init — the namespace initialization callback (@ref qore_module_ns_init_t)
    - \c del — the module cleanup/destructor callback (@ref qore_module_delete_t)
    - \c parse_cmd — optional parse command handler (@ref qore_module_parse_cmd_t); set to \c nullptr if not used
    - \c license — the license type (default: @ref QL_MIT); set to @ref QL_GPL if the module links with GPL code
    - \c license_str — the license as a string (e.g. \c "MIT", \c "LGPL", \c "GPL")
    - \c dependencies — a list of other binary module names this module depends on (\c strvec_t)
    - \c info — an optional @ref QoreHashNode pointer with extra module metadata (default: \c nullptr)
    - \c functional_domains — a bitmask of \c QDOM_* flags for sandboxing (default: \c 0 = no restrictions)

    The \c functional_domains field allows the %Qore library to enforce sandboxing restrictions.  For example, a
    module that accesses the network and databases should set:
    @code{.cpp}
    mod_info.functional_domains = QDOM_DATABASE | QDOM_NETWORK;
    @endcode

    @note The @ref QoreModuleInfo struct uses default member initializers, so fields like \c license (defaults to
    @ref QL_MIT), \c functional_domains (defaults to \c 0), and callback pointers (default to \c nullptr) only need
    to be set if non-default values are required.  This extensible design means new fields can be added with sensible
    defaults without breaking existing modules.

    @subsection module_functions Module Callback Functions

    @par Module Initialization — @ref qore_module_init_t

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_init_t)(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    @endcode

    Called when the module is first loaded.  Use this to create namespaces, register classes, constants, and functions.
    The @ref QoreModuleInitContext struct provides the \c path to the module file.  Report errors by raising
    exceptions on \c xsink; if any exception is raised, the module load will fail.

    @par Namespace Initialization — @ref qore_module_ns_init_t

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_ns_init_t)(QoreNamespace* root_ns, QoreNamespace* qore_ns, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    @endcode

    Called when the module's namespace additions need to be provisioned to a @ref QoreProgram object — either when
    the module is first loaded or when a program requests the module's feature after it was already loaded by another
    program.  Typically this copies the module's reference namespace into the Qore namespace tree.  Report errors on
    \c xsink.

    @par Module Cleanup — @ref qore_module_delete_t

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_delete_t)();
    @endcode

    Called when the module is unloaded (typically when the %Qore library shuts down).  Free any resources allocated
    during initialization.

    @par Parse Command Handler — @ref qore_module_parse_cmd_t (optional)

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_parse_cmd_t)(const QoreString& cmd, ExceptionSink* xsink);
    @endcode

    If set, allows the module to handle parse-time commands issued via the \c %module-cmd directive.  Set to
    \c nullptr in the description function if not used (this is the default).

    @subsection module_example Complete Example

    The following is a minimal but complete example based on the \c astparser module:

    @code{.cpp}
    #include "qore/Qore.h"

    static void mymodule_module_init(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    static void mymodule_module_ns_init(QoreNamespace* rns, QoreNamespace* qns, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    static void mymodule_module_delete();

    // Module description function — the entry point discovered by the Qore library
    extern "C" DLLEXPORT void mymodule_qore_module_desc(QoreModuleInfo& mod_info) {
        mod_info.name = "mymodule";
        mod_info.version = PACKAGE_VERSION;
        mod_info.desc = "Example Qore module";
        mod_info.author = "Author Name";
        mod_info.url = "http://example.com";
        mod_info.api_major = QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR;
        mod_info.api_minor = QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR;
        mod_info.init = mymodule_module_init;
        mod_info.ns_init = mymodule_module_ns_init;
        mod_info.del = mymodule_module_delete;
        mod_info.license = QL_MIT;
        mod_info.license_str = "MIT";
        // set functional domains for sandboxing if the module accesses restricted resources
        // mod_info.functional_domains = QDOM_NETWORK;
    }

    // Reference namespace — initialized once, copied for each QoreProgram
    static QoreNamespace MyModuleNS("Qore::mymodule");

    static void mymodule_module_init(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink) {
        // Register classes, constants, and functions in the namespace
        MyModuleNS.addSystemClass(initMyClass(MyModuleNS));
        init_my_constants(MyModuleNS);
    }

    static void mymodule_module_ns_init(QoreNamespace* rns, QoreNamespace* qns, ExceptionSink& xsink) {
        // Copy the module's namespace into the Qore namespace
        qns->addNamespace(MyModuleNS.copy());
    }

    static void mymodule_module_delete() {
        // Free any resources allocated during initialization
    }
    @endcode

    @note The module name (feature name) set via \c mod_info.name is used to uniquely identify the module.  If a
    module providing feature \c "widget" is already loaded, another module claiming the same feature cannot be loaded.

    @note The \c api_major and \c api_minor fields are used to verify that the module is compatible with the %Qore
    library loading it.  Always set these to @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR and @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR.

    @note Classes must not be created more than once, because each Qore class gets a unique ID at creation time.
    Initialize classes in the \c init callback and use \c copy() in the \c ns_init callback.
 */
